he street or in cafes or  whatever you  you come across conversations don't you  you've you're not there at the beginning  of them  you come in sort of halfway through and  i've done so many shapes with plays  i've done almost all of them and very  rarely does the scene  start at the top of somebody beginning  to say something often people come on  stage and they're already in  conversation  because that's what we do that's how we  hear life we hear life through fragments  and snatches of conversation  so it seemed to me a very good way to  begin  and so you imagine people together and  what they're talking about  their need to speak i mean why do i need  to tell you this now  you know i have a need to share this  with you so  that's what you want to find as a  playwright or as a writer what's the  need for that person to say what they're  saying  and then what's the other person's  reaction to that need  are they going to be challenged by it  are they going to add to it are they  going to disagree or agree  so you start with all of that now later  on down the line  comes all the difficulties because then  you want to  be sure that you have a story that has  action and that it  really is compelling and moving the  story forward  is the mark of a great writer and i  don't profess to be a great writer yet  what's your what's your process of  editing then  i'm not bad at editing  because as a composer particularly for  the theater you've got to you can't be  proud about it you can't be  egocentric a director says you're going  to have to cut two minutes  because the show's too long  and this cue you know we don't need so  much  now two minutes of music is a lot it can  take  i don't know three days four days to  write two minutes of music  um so you have to kind of swallow your  pride and go  okay the good of the show i'll do it and  so you get into that frame of mind  so when you look at editing you've got  to  not be precious you've got to say what  serves a story what's the audience  understanding  how am i going to give them this story  in the most economical  and powerful way and often you have to  just jettison  all your best bits you think throw them  overboard and actually it's usually the  better for it  okay once you've gone through that  approach so you've been through that  process  you've written something it's there what  unique qualities  does live performance bring well  actually  it's not even in performance it's in  rehearsal because  a play is just that it's playful  it's not a piece of literature and so  often i read scripts that come through  the door  or you read plays and they're so  literary  and players were never supposed to be  that they're supposed to be  real you know how people really behave  so you right you may write it but then  you get it into rehearsal  and you hear it coming out of the actors  mouths and it's that awful kind of  cringe moment you think  this is not how that person should speak  i mean it's way too many words and if  it's a good actor and they usually are  um they'll have great suggestions how  about if i just switch  the sentence round and i start with what  you've written at the end of it  you know for example um  do you think we should go on holiday  next week  it's perhaps more natural to go been  thinking next week  do you think we should go on holiday but  as a writer you might not write it like  that you see what i mean  so it's another step in the process it's  very very exciting  for a writer to meet the actor in  rehearsal and then the next step  is in front of an audience who have not  had  three to six weeks rehearsal with you  they don't know the story they're not  invested  they've paid their money but they want  they want to give you they  you know they want the story from you so  i tend to watch them  quite carefully and um  in previews we've got usually a luxury  whereby we can  cut and change and develop and do a few  rewrites and  it's always the audience i watch in  previews what do you learn from them  i learn where their attention is and  their focus  if they start sort of looking down at  their watch you know i mean that's  that's a kind of bad moment you know i  want them to be leaning forward in their  chairs i want them to be  interested sometimes you can see where  they're confused  where they're watching something and  they're interested then suddenly  you can see that they're thinking you  don't want an audience to do that  you want them to be um you want to be  ahead of them  not behind them you want them to you  want to be leading an audience forward  so they're  excited to know what's going to happen  next  um what are the capabilities  of music in theater  you know i've changed my mind about that  so many times over the course of  my career starting out i used to think  that it was so important and that i  laced  everything with music cues you know and  i kind of could almost told the story  with music you could have taken the  words away and  you know that in my mind um  it varies i think with let's let's look  at shakespeare  i think you shouldn't underscore  shakespeare at all  and yet i did for years and years and  probably until about five years ago  i was doing beautiful cues that told you  how to feel and think  now i think the words are such powerful  pieces of music in themselves you don't  want to get in the way  shakespeare knows when to have music and  when not  and so gradually you begin to have a  sense of where music is helpful and  where it's not  um other pieces that are not  so text-based become very powerful  with great choices of music whether it's  extant music that you  love to use like a piece of philip glass  or something  or whether you get a composer to write  something very specific  for you so it depends but i think  for my sense um  less is always more that's quite a big  shift to have  to have gone from underscoring  shakespeare for the globe  to thinking it shouldn't be there 