n drink you  know it's  i think what's dangerous is the  commercial road is going to be so  exclusive  there are less and less people that are  going to be able to go to  west end theaters even with somebody  like sonia friedman who  gives you know what 25  or 100 tickets at an incredibly cheap  price if you turn up on the day  even with something like that it's going  to become so exclusive  that it's really not fired by  normal people in the community it's um  it's only a very small part of society  that that can take part in that kind of  theater  so i don't know where we're going with  it i'd love to see  a middle ground where there was funding  as  a long time ago in my life there was you  could take a risk  and you wouldn't lose your shirt um you  would have the right to what you would  call  fail although i don't think there's any  sense of failure  in experimentation but you could  try different things and i think that's  how we grow  and how we learn about art and culture  if you don't have access to any kind of  funding  and you can only do romeo and juliet  because you need the footfall  to pay for it where are we going  and what what are we doing why are we  doing theater at all  so i am it's my personal belief  shared by not not only of you that the  government should stop spending billions  on  defense and actually spend a little bit  of money on art and culture  i think that would be really great i  agree  um can we can sorry your attitude  towards failure  sounds interesting can you talk a bit  more about that maybe  i think that our education system um  programs us to fail to feel that we fail  um from an early age i think it is very  poor  um we're  we're asked to to take part in exams  that have a structure  very early on as children we're asked to  stop playing  and to start learning you know which  means we have to work  for something that will get marked  ticked  um we have scores and this sets up  a feeling that we we've got to do it  right  y